  "response": "When an accused scientist and a whistleblower offer incompatible narratives, investigators do not adjudicate between personalities. They treat each account as a hypothesis that makes testable predictions about the physical and digital record, and they ask which predictions the surviving evidence bears out. A misconduct finding requires a showing of intent, knowledge, or recklessness, so the decisive question is rarely whether the data are wrong. It is whether the pattern of wrongness fits an innocent mechanism or a purposeful one. A representative case shows how this reasoning proceeds. Dr. Mara Lin published a study reporting a new biomarker for early pancreatic cancer, and only after that publication did a postdoctoral researcher in Dr. Lin's lab file a whistleblower complaint about the study's data.\n\nBecause Dr. Lin had published the study, the whistleblower alleged that several Western blot images in the study were duplicated across different experiments. Although that allegation was specific and checkable, Dr. Lin stated that the duplicated images resulted from an honest file-labelling mistake. Investigators then weighed the standing of each party. The whistleblower was a co-author who had personally generated all of the disputed images. By contrast, forensic image analysis identified identical background noise patterns in panels labelled as separate experiments. That noise evidence is significant because no two independent exposures of a membrane reproduce the same stochastic speckle. Identical noise therefore shows that a single image was reused, though it cannot by itself say why. Dr. Lin's labelling explanation predicts a static archive in which files were merely misnamed. Whereas her account implies such an archive, file metadata showed that the original image files were modified after the manuscript was submitted.\n\nAt this stage the investigators framed the remaining possibilities with care. At least one of the following must hold, perhaps both: the raw data files underlying the figures were altered after collection, or the figure panels in the study were assembled from mislabelled images. The first possibility points toward falsification. The second is consistent with carelessness. Distinguishing them would normally depend on contemporaneous records. Dr. Lin's original laboratory notebooks were never produced to the investigators. In other words, the investigators never received the original laboratory notebooks from Dr. Lin. That absence indicates that, under United States federal policy, honest error is explicitly listed as a form of research misconduct. Committees accordingly treat a respondent's failure to supply primary records as weighing heavily against an exculpatory narrative that only those records could corroborate.\n\nThe evidentiary picture thus converged on a single reading. As shown by the mislabelled images from which the figure panels were assembled, the university investigation committee concluded that the duplicated images were deliberately fabricated and did not result from a labelling mistake. That conclusion settles the tension between the whistleblower's allegation and Dr. Lin's explanation. Duplication was not in dispute, and once the committee rejected the labelling account, the claim of honest error no longer stood. The Office of Research Integrity ultimately determined that Dr. Lin knowingly falsified data in the published study, and that federal determination caused the committee's finding of deliberate fabrication. The two bodies apply a preponderance-of-the-evidence standard. Neither requires a confession, and both infer intent from convergent circumstantial evidence: reused noise signatures, post-submission file modification, and missing primary records.\n\nThe final consequence for the scientific literature depends on the publisher rather than the investigators. Either the journal retracted the study, or the study has never been retracted by the journal. The two cannot both be true, and one of them must hold. Journals sometimes act on an institutional report within weeks, and sometimes they delay pending appeals or legal review. The general method, however, does not change with the outcome. Investigators translate each competing account into concrete predictions about images, metadata, and records. They test those predictions against forensic analysis. They then treat unexplained gaps in the respondent's documentation as evidence in their own right. Conflicting testimony is resolved not by deciding whom to believe, but by determining which account the artifacts can sustain.",
